= Jaggery (Guḍa)

_Ayurvedic status: fully classical — guḍa is one of the most-cited substances in the saṃhitās, treated as the canonical Vāta-pacifying sweet and the principal_ anupāna _(adjuvant vehicle) for many medicines; the Sanskrit canon distinguishes a full spectrum of unrefined-to-refined cane-sugar products. Chinese: classical — 紅糖 (hóngtáng, "red sugar") is the Chinese equivalent, fully treated in dietetic literature with a particular emphasis on gynaecological and postpartum use. Greco-Roman: not classical — cane sugar reached the Mediterranean only in the medieval period via Arab agronomy. The Brazilian / Latin-American equivalent is_ rapadura / panela / piloncillo _— see_ rapadura.typ _for the New-World register; this entry treats the substance under its classical Indian name._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Botanical:* _Saccharum officinarum_ L. (sugarcane); the foodstuff is the *whole-cane juice concentrated by evaporation*, with the molasses fraction *retained* (the defining process distinguishing jaggery from refined white sugar). Sometimes also produced from palm sap (Borassus, Phoenix, Cocos) — the *palm jaggery*, treated as a slightly distinct drug; the cane-jaggery is the dominant form. _Saccharum_ < Greek _sákcharon_ < Sanskrit _śarkarā_ — see rapadura entry.

*English:* _Jaggery_, from Portuguese _jágara_, from Konkani / Tamil _ċakkarai_ (சக்கரை, "sugar"), from Sanskrit _śarkarā_. The English word entered via the Portuguese trading vocabulary in Goa and Kerala from the 16th c.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "jaggery, n."); the Konkani-Portuguese-English chain is the standard etymology.]

*Hindi / Modern Indic:* _गुड़_ (_guṛ_, also Romanized as _gur_) — the principal modern term in North India; _bellam_ (బెల్లం) in Telugu, _vellam_ (வெல்லம்) in Tamil, _bella_ (ಬೆಲ್ಲ) in Kannada — all from the Dravidian root for "sugar."

*Sanskrit:* The canonical text-tradition distinguishes a full *sugar-product spectrum* by degree of clarification — *the most-detailed classical sugar pharmacology in any tradition*:
- *इक्षु (_ikṣu_)* — sugarcane, the plant.
- *इक्षुरस (_ikṣu-rasa_)* — fresh cane juice; the unprocessed liquid; treated as a drug.
- *फाणित (_phāṇita_)* — partially evaporated cane juice (thick syrup); the first concentration stage.
- *गुड (_guḍa_)* — *the principal unrefined cane sugar*; the modern jaggery in classical reference; subdivided into _puṛāṇa-guḍa_ (aged) and _nava-guḍa_ (fresh).
- *मत्स्यण्डी (_matsyaṇḍī_)* — a thicker, mid-crystallization stage between guḍa and śarkarā.
- *खण्ड (_khaṇḍa_)* — "lump sugar" — partially clarified, lighter than guḍa.
- *शर्करा (_śarkarā_)* — *crystallized sugar*, more refined; treated as a more cooling and refined sugar.
- *सिता (_sitā_)* — *the most refined, white crystallized sugar*; the apex of the refinement series; classically cooling and Pitta-pacifying.
- *मिश्री (_miśrī_)* — rock sugar, the modern Indian / Persian rock-sugar form.

Each of these grades has a distinct dravyaguna placement; the Sanskrit canon's *vīrya-shifts-with-refinement* doctrine is precise: *unrefined warming → refined cooling*, with each grade between recalibrated. Modern white sugar is the apex of _sitā_ in this scheme — the most cooling, the most refined, and (in modern terms) the most pharmacologically depleted.#footnote[Sugar-product spectrum: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_annapānavidhi_, sweet-substance section); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 45; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Ikṣu-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba); Sharangadhara Saṃhitā for processing-method details. The vīrya-shift-with-refinement doctrine is universal across the saṃhitā tradition.]

*Chinese:* 紅糖 (hóngtáng, "red sugar") — see rapadura.typ for the parallel CCM treatment.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

紅糖 (see also rapadura entry): warm (溫), sweet (甘), Liver-Spleen-Stomach. 補中緩肝, 散寒活血, 補血, 健脾胃. The canonical 紅糖薑湯 (red-sugar-ginger soup) for menstrual cramps and postpartum recovery. Contraindicated in damp-heat and diabetes.

The closest direct CCM analogue of Indian guḍa is 紅糖; the Chinese reading converges precisely with the Ayurvedic _guḍa_ reading on the *warming, blood-supportive, postpartum-tonic* profile.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

*Rasa:* Madhura (sweet) — dominant.
*Guṇa:* Guru (heavy), Snigdha (unctuous).
*Vīrya:* *Uṣṇa (warming)* — *the defining feature of guḍa relative to refined śarkarā*. The warmer character is what makes guḍa the *Vāta-pacifying* sweet, while śarkarā is the *Pitta-pacifying* sweet.
*Vipāka:* Madhura (sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of guḍa: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Ikṣu-varga_; the unique uṣṇa vīrya of guḍa among classical sweets is universally noted.]

*Prabhāva:* *Yogavāhī* — *carries other drugs to their target*; the prabhāva-level _anupāna_ property of guḍa is universally cited in classical commentary as making it the standard vehicle for _vaṭīs_ (pills), _avalehyas_ (electuaries), _ariṣṭas_ (fermented preparations), and many other compound preparations. *Aged guḍa (purāṇa-guḍa)* is specifically *digestive and gentler* than fresh guḍa; the aged form is the preferred medicinal form.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta (the snigdha-uṣṇa-madhura profile); aggravates Kapha and Pitta in excess (the heaviness, the warming).

*Karma:*
- _Balya, bṛṃhaṇa_ — strengthening, nourishing.
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic.
- _Yogavāhī_ — carries co-administered drugs.
- _Rakta-vardhana_ — blood-supportive (the iron / mineral content; cross-system convergence with the Chinese 補血 reading).
- _Vāta-anulomana_ — relieves Vāta gas / constipation (gentle laxative — guḍa with warm milk is a classical mild laxative).
- _Mūtrala_ — modest diuretic.
- _Aphrodisiac, postpartum tonic, convalescent food_ — the warming-sweet register makes it suitable for these states. The classical postpartum recommendation of *guḍa + ghee + warming spices + milk* (the _pañjīrī_ tradition of North India, the _karupatti coffee_ tradition of South India) is universal across regional South Asian cultures.
- *Used in:* Vāta disorders, _kṣaya_ (wasting), postpartum, _kāsa-śvāsa_ (cold-pattern cough and dyspnea), anaemia (modest), as the universal _anupāna_ for medicinal preparations.

=== Viruddha

- *(S) Conditional:* _amlapitta_ (hyperacidity with Pitta), _prameha_ (diabetes — guḍa is contraindicated in classical _prameha_), _kṛmi-roga_ (worms — sweet substances feed parasites by classical reading; modern microbiology partially confirms in the case of dysbiosis), heavy Kapha excess.
- *(S) Specific saṃyoga viruddha:* *guḍa with fish* in some classical commentary — a saṃyoga-viruddha; modern relevance unclear.
- *(M) Modern:* *diabetes / metabolic syndrome* — guḍa is sucrose-predominant; the popular "healthy sugar" framing overstates the case (similar to rapadura — see that entry). The mineral / iron / molasses contribution is real but modest. Children: cariogenicity.
- *Lead contamination:* documented in low-quality Indian commercial guḍa from some sources; reputable producers test.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Cane sugar is canonical in Tibb under the name_ sukkar _— the Arabic-Persian word borrowed from Sanskrit_ śarkarā _that spread west to give all European sugar-words. The Tibb tradition recognizes the full unrefined-to-refined spectrum. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ _and_ jaggery.typ.

*Arabic:* سكر (_sukkar_) — generic sugar; سكر أحمر (_sukkar aḥmar_, "red sugar") — unrefined whole-cane sugar / jaggery equivalent; سكر طبرزد (_sukkar ṭabarzad_) — rock sugar; سكر سليمانى (_sukkar sulaymānī_) — Solomon's sugar, fine refined; دبس (_dibs_) — sugarcane molasses / date syrup. Persian: شکر (_shakar_); گُر (_gur_, jaggery — South Asian Persian usage). Urdu/Hakeemi: گڑ (_guṛ_, jaggery); شکر (_shakar_); قند (_qand_, lump sugar).

*Mizāj:* the unrefined / jaggery / molasses-retained form *sukkar aḥmar / sukkar khām*: *Hot and Moist in the 1st–2nd degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _sukkar_). The refined white sugar (*sukkar ṭabarzad*) is *cooler* (some sources Cold-Moist 1st). The vīrya-shifts-with-refinement Ayurvedic doctrine is closely paralleled.

*Af'āl:* *Mughadhdhī* (nourishing); *mulayyin* (gentle laxative); *muraṭṭib* (humectant); *muḥsin li-l-balgham* (modifies phlegm); *munaffith* (expectorant); *muqawwī al-mi'da*; *mufariḥ* (mood-elevating, modest); *yogavāhī* / *muwāṣil* — *carrier* — the universal _anupāna_ function preserved in Unani; *muqawwī al-rūḥ* in compound preparations.

*Istemālāt:* convalescence; postpartum recovery (the cross-cultural Ayurvedic-Chinese-Tibb convergent register); chronic cough with thin phlegm; debility; gastric weakness; as universal vehicle for Tibb syrups (*sharbat* class is sugar-based) and electuaries (*maʿjūn* class is sugar/honey-based).

*Mawāne':* heavy phlegmatic conditions; bilious / hot-pattern excess (for the more warming forms); diabetes (modern, absolute); obesity.

*Muṣliḥ:* sour fruits; vinegar; cooling herbs.

*Badal:* honey for unrefined; date syrup (*dibs al-tamr*); raisin syrup for sweetening function.

*Tibb formulas:* *Sharbat (شربت)* — the syrup class is sugar-based; *Maʿjūn (معجون)* — electuary class uses sugar/honey base; *Khamīrah (خميرة)* — fermented preparation, sugar-based; *Qand (قند)* — lump-sugar preparations; the Mughal-Hakeemi *halwā* and *muraba* (preserve) traditions are central Unani sweet-preparation classes.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _sukkar_ entry; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*, _sukkar_ entry (with comprehensive grade-distinction discussion); *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III.#footnote[Tibb sukkar: the Sanskrit-Arabic-European etymological transmission of the sugar word is one of the cleanest cross-cultural transfers in pharmacology; the Tibb grade-distinction parallels the Ayurvedic spectrum closely.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *紅糖薑湯 (Hóngtáng Jiāngtāng, "Red-Sugar Ginger Soup")* — the canonical postpartum and menstrual-cramp folk preparation.
- *In compound formulas* — 紅糖 functions as a sweetening and harmonizing vehicle.

=== Ayurveda

- *Guḍa as universal _anupāna_* — accompanies an enormous fraction of classical formulas as the vehicle; the texts repeatedly specify dose as "with old guḍa" (_purāṇa-guḍa-yukta_).
- *Guḍa-pāka preparations* — concentrated guḍa-based electuaries, the substrate for many _avalehas_ (Chyavanprash, etc.).
- *Pañjīrī (पंजीरी)* — North-Indian postpartum tonic: roasted whole-wheat flour, ghee, guḍa, dried fruits, gum (gond) — slow-cooked sweet for new mothers; one of the most universal postpartum foods in north India.
- *Gajaka, chikki, til-laddu, gud-paṭṭī* — guḍa-based winter sweets, traditional in north India for Lohri / Makar Sankranti and winter generally; consistent with the warming dravyaguna placement.
- *Til-guḍ (sesame-jaggery)* — the canonical north Indian winter pairing for Vāta-pacification.
- *Kṣīrapāka with guḍa* — guḍa-milk decoction for postpartum and convalescent use.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

No classical Greco-Roman pharmacy of cane sugar (cane sugar was unknown to the Mediterranean classical world). Reached Europe via Arab agronomy in the medieval period; the European sugar tradition is post-classical. The European sugar pharmacy of the 16th–19th c. (sugar as drug — _saccharum officinarum_, the pharmacy-store sugar) used refined and crystallized forms more than unrefined jaggery; the Indian jaggery is *not* a European pharmacy substance.#footnote[European sugar history: Mintz, _Sweetness and Power_; standard pharmacy history. The Indian jaggery tradition has no direct European parallel until the modern wholefoods movement.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Composition, jaggery, approximate per 100 g — verify against published surveys; values vary by region and processing.

- Energy ~380 kcal
- *Sucrose ~70–80%*
- Glucose + fructose (reducing sugars) ~10–15%
- Water ~3–10%
- Iron ~10–15 mg/100 g — substantially higher than white sugar
- Calcium ~80–100 mg/100 g
- Potassium, magnesium, zinc — modest
- Trace B vitamins
- Polyphenols / antioxidant fraction — small but measurable

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Glycemic index ~65* — broadly similar to white sugar; the popular "low-GI sugar" claim is overstated.
- *Iron contribution:* modest but real; in iron-deficient populations the daily 10–20 g of jaggery used in cooking can provide ~1–2 mg of dietary iron (modest contribution).
- *Cross-system convergence:* the classical _guḍa_ + 紅糖 + Brazilian _rapadura_ + Latin-American _panela_ tradition converges on *whole-cane sugar as warming, mineral-supportive, postpartum-and-convalescent tonic*; the modern molasses chemistry partially supports the classical reading.
- *Postpartum / lactation:* the iron + caloric-density + warming-character justifies the cross-cultural postpartum-tonic use modestly; the practice is reasonable on classical and modern grounds.
- *Diabetes:* contraindicated as for any sugar.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* No toxicological upper limit per se; standard added-sugar guidance applies (WHO ≤25–50 g/day total added sugars). The medicinal _anupāna_ dose is typically small (a few grams with a pill or powder); culinary use is the larger source. Risk groups: diabetes, metabolic syndrome, dental caries, children.

